# Partner-commercial challenge: proving the non-center repayment case

**Prepared October 8, 2026.** This review corrects one risk in a center-only reading of the plan: provider licenses, church programs, training, support, and merchandise can become repayment sources. They should be modeled as separate, evidence-gated businesses rather than ignored or blended into tuition.

## The strongest B2B path

The first outside sale should be a paid, four-week implementation pilot with one owner-operated daycare or home provider. It should include a defined age group, room or zone, bilingual family starter materials, staff onboarding, parent orientation, a support channel, and a 30-day acceptance report. The provider pays a deposit against a written scope; monthly service begins only after delivery acceptance and a working hosted service. This creates evidence for setup revenue, recurring support, implementation hours, gross contribution, and renewal.

The second path is a church or community program. It needs its own offer because a church may host the companyâ€™s center, purchase a weekday program, run a weekend family program, or license materials to a ministry. The existing host church is not automatically a customer. Separate the host-facility relationship from an external church customer, with different agreements, pricing, data responsibilities, insurance, safeguarding, storage, and termination rights.

The third path is a multi-site license after one-site delivery is repeatable. A multi-site buyer should pay a portfolio setup fee, per-site implementation, and recurring organization license with named sites, role access, reporting, support limits, and renewal. Do not offer â€œunlimitedâ€ support or children before the team has measured support hours and data boundaries.

## Which residual streams deserve investor attention

**Provider recurring license and support â€” highest priority.** This is the cleanest residual stream because it can renew after the room kit is installed. The price must cover hosting, curriculum updates, bilingual review, support, backups, payment processing, and implementation follow-up. Report active paid accounts, collected monthly recurring revenue, gross contribution after direct support, churn, renewal, and average support hours. Proposals and unpaid invoices are pipeline, not recurring revenue.

**Church or ministry license â€” attractive but scope-sensitive.** A church can buy a weekday childcare package, a weekend adaptation, a family learning series, or a branded room. These are different products. A weekend program may need shorter sessions, volunteer training, pickup procedures, child-safety controls, age-group changes, and a different insurance arrangement. A ministry license must state whether the church may reproduce materials, use the characters, operate without the portal, or serve children outside the original age range. Quote each adaptation rather than hiding it inside a low monthly fee.

**Training and professional development â€” useful expansion revenue.** Advanced seats, leadership training, trainer certification, and renewal workshops can be billable. Foundations and Home Educator may be included in a package to reduce adoption friction. Do not count included training again as paid seat revenue. Track seats sold, completion, instructor hours, materials, travel, refunds, and whether training increases renewal. Trainer Academy is a separate service only when the company can supervise practicum and protect the curriculum.

**Classroom kits and branded merchandise â€” upfront cash and repeat orders, not passive residual.** A new classroom kit can produce setup revenue. Replacement posters, carpets, signs, books, apparel, seasonal materials, and expansion kits can create repeat orders. They require supplier quotes, deposits, minimum quantities, freight, safety and labeling records, inventory controls, return policy, artwork approval, and fulfillment capacity. Custom carpets should be made to order with a customer deposit so the company does not borrow to speculate on inventory.

**Employer or sponsored-family programs â€” later channel.** An employer may sponsor family materials or childcare support, but the buyer, child-data boundaries, reimbursement terms, and renewal are different from a provider license. Keep it as a tested channel hypothesis until an employer signs a scope and pays.

## Model assumptions that need challenge

The current planning case of two Home packages and three Center Starter packages produces $11,975 of proposed setup list-price revenue and $865 of proposed monthly list-price receipts. It is a reasonable sensitivity case, but it should not be presented as a sales forecast until management can show a named pipeline, decision-maker, conversion rate, implementation capacity, and payment path.

The five-account case also understates the operational question: if every account needs hands-on onboarding, bilingual review, room design, support, and replacement materials, the $865 monthly gross receipt may not cover direct service time. Add a per-account cost schedule with setup hours, training hours, travel, hosting, support, payment fees, content updates, and replacement allowance. Calculate contribution before sales and overhead. An account that renews but consumes founder-level support can still weaken debt service.

The model should include four provider cases:

| Case | What it tests | Revenue treatment |
|---|---|---|
| Pilot | One paid home or center implementation | Deposit and final payment only after acceptance; no recurring revenue before service start |
| Base | Five paid accounts over twelve months | Setup receipts by delivery month; monthly receipts only after settlement; direct support cost included |
| Partner | One church or community license | Separate scope and fee; no assumed children, referrals, or host-facility subsidy |
| Scale | Multi-site organization | Per-site implementation plus portfolio license; requires contract, staffing and data controls |

For downside, delay provider billing by 90 days, halve conversion, add one implementation month, assume no merchandise sale, and include support labor. If the note remains serviceable under that case, the B2B channel is helping repayment. If it does not, label provider revenue as upside and use staged draws.

## Recommended acquisition offer

Offer a **Founding Provider Pilot** to three carefully selected local providers: one home daycare, one small center, and one church/community program only if the safeguarding and operating scope are appropriate. Each pays a defined setup deposit, signs a four-week scope, receives a room/lesson/family launch, and agrees to a 30-day review. In exchange for participating in the measurement process, the provider receives a locked first-year price and a defined support allowance. Do not promise lifetime pricing, unlimited revisions, custom development, or exclusivity.

The pilot scorecard should measure: staff onboarding completion; weekly plan usage; parent orientation and family engagement; support hours; portal acceptance; bilingual content review; attendance or routine adherence where appropriate; first invoice settlement; implementation gross contribution; and renewal decision. A customer quote is useful, but the bank-reconciled payment and delivery acceptance are the evidence.

The offer should have three clear steps: **setup**, **service**, and **expansion**. Setup covers approved materials, implementation, and training. Service covers monthly curriculum updates, family materials, support, and hosted portal access when live. Expansion covers additional zones, sites, training seats, and merchandise. This prevents a low monthly license from quietly carrying every future promise.

## Church-specific safeguards

Use separate documents for: host facility; church as paying program customer; and church as referral or community partner. A church program must identify who employs or supervises staff, who maintains attendance and authorized pickup records, who owns child data, who handles incidents and mandated reporting, what background checks apply, where materials are stored, and what happens when the ministry or facility relationship ends. Weekend or midweek adaptations need their own staffing and safeguarding review. A churchâ€™s mission alignment is a sales advantage, not a substitute for a contract or license.

## How to describe leverage without overstating it

The leverage is that one approved curriculum and brand system can be delivered through multiple provider types, with setup, recurring service, training, room materials, replacement orders, and future site expansion. The operational leverage is real only if the company can implement the next provider without founder-only labor. Build a delivery checklist, train a certified implementer, use reusable materials, and record direct hours before claiming scalable margin.

The fair investor wording is: **â€œThe center is our proving ground and the provider business is the expansion path. Tuition supports the operating site; paid provider setup, recurring support, training, and made-to-order materials create separate repayment sources once collected and measured. We will not count a proposal, an unpaid invoice, an unaccepted delivery, or catalog margin as cash available for debt.â€**

## Lender bonus and ownership boundary

The cleanest instrument remains a fixed-interest business note with staged draws. If a higher tier needs additional upside, a finite, capped, time-limited cash bonus can be evaluated by counsel. It should be tied to defined collected provider-service revenue, exclude center tuition, merchandise pass-through, refunds, taxes, and uncollected invoices, end within a fixed period, and have a hard dollar cap. It must confer no equity, conversion right, voting right, permanent ownership, or control. A fixed-dollar milestone bonus may be easier to explain than a percentage of revenue, but both require counsel to review lending, tax, securities, and usury treatment.

## Questions an investor will ask

â€œAre churches paying customers?â€ The answer must identify each church relationship as host, customer, referral partner, or pilot; no category should be implied from another.

â€œWhat renews?â€ Provider access, support, curriculum updates, family materials, and approved portal service renew. Carpets and first-run kits do not renew monthly; they generate new orders or replacement sales.

â€œDoes training produce recurring income?â€ Usually no. It is a billable setup or seat product unless a renewal workshop or annual certification is contracted. Included training must not be counted twice.

â€œCan five providers pay the note?â€ Not yet proven. Show collections and contribution after direct delivery/support costs, then combine that evidence with center cash flow.

â€œWhat happens if the Hub is delayed?â€ Pause recurring billing, use the manual licensed-center workflow only under a defined support scope, and move the provider tranche until hosted acceptance is complete. The current website is a demonstration; root verification says the local Hub is internally complete and tested but not deployed, and the store payment configuration is not currently ready. Describe the store as an inspectable catalog and the provider service as staged, rather than using a blanket claim that every order path is closed.

â€œWhat turns this into a large company?â€ Repeatable provider acquisition, multi-site licenses, low-touch implementation, strong renewal, measured outcomes, integration with incumbent administrative tools, and disciplined support economics. More product categories alone do not create scale.

## Required package addition

Add a B2B revenue bridge after the center bridge: provider setup collections; monthly license collections; church program collections; training seats; new kits; replacement merchandise; direct delivery costs; support labor; payment fees; refunds; contribution; and cash available for debt. Show zero, base, and downside cases. Keep the center, provider, church, training, and merchandise cost centers separate so residual revenue strengthens the case without masking a center shortfall.

